{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A9RgYi6bfd8yiF1jGJ9HE8","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have a comprehensive view of the patterns. Let me create a summary report.\n\n## Summary: --help and --version Flag Implementation Patterns\n\nBased on my analysis of the cmd/*/main.go files in the repository, here are the established patterns for handling --help and --version flags:\n\n### Pattern 1: Constant Help and Version Text (Most Common)\n\nAll commands define help and version strings as package-level constants:\n\n```go\nconst helpText = `Usage: head [OPTION]... [FILE]...\nPrint the first 10 lines of each FILE to standard output.\n...\n      --help        display this help and exit\n      --version     output version information and exit\n`\n\nconst versionText = `head (go-unix-utils) dev\n`\n```\n\n### Pattern 2: Flag Handling Structure\n\nCommands use two flag parsing functions:\n\n1. **parseLongFlag(flag string, remaining []string, opts *options)** - handles --flags\n2. **parseShortFlags(flags string, remaining []string, opts *options)** - handles -f style flags\n\n### Pattern 3: Help and Version Implementation\n\nIn parseLongFlag function:\n\n```go\nfunc parseLongFlag(flag string, remaining []string, opts *options) (int, error) {\n\tswitch {\n\tcase flag == \"--help\":\n\t\tfmt.Fprint(os.Stdout, helpText)\n\t\tos.Exit(0)\n\t\treturn 0, nil\n\tcase flag == \"--version\":\n\t\tfmt.Fprint(os.Stdout, versionText)\n\t\tos.Exit(0)\n\t\treturn 0, nil\n\t// ... other flags\n\t}\n}\n```\n\n**Key characteristics:**\n- Prints to `os.Stdout` using `fmt.Fprint()` (not `fmt.Println()`, which adds a newline)\n- Calls `os.Exit(0)` immediately after printing\n- Returns `0, nil` (dead code, but keeps the compiler happy with return signatures)\n- No short forms like `-h` or `-v` are implemented (version uses `--version` only)\n\n### Pattern 4: Error Handling for Unrecognized Flags\n\nUnrecognized options follow this pattern:\n\n```go\ndefault:\n\treturn 0, fmt.Errorf(\"unrecognized option '%s'\", flag)\n```\n\nThe error is then handled in main():\n```go\nopts, files, err := parseArgs(os.Args[1:])\nif err != nil {\n\tfmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, \"head: %s\\n\", err)\n\tfmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, \"Try 'head --help' for more information.\\n\")\n\tos.Exit(1)\n}\n```\n\n### Pattern 5: Version String Format\n\nVersion text follows a consistent format:\n```\n<cmd> (go-unix-utils) dev\n```\n\nExamples seen:\n- `head (go-unix-utils) dev`\n- `ls (go-unix-utils) dev`\n- `pwd (go-unix-utils) dev`\n- `basename (go-unix-utils) dev`\n- `seq (go-unix-utils) dev`\n\n### Pattern 6: Exit Code Convention\n\n- `--help` and `--version`: Exit with code **0** (success)\n- Invalid flags/options: Exit with code **1** (error)\n- Invalid flag characters in short flags: Exit with code **1**\n\n### Pattern 7: Error Messages for Invalid Flags\n\nShort flag errors:\n```go\ndefault:\n\tfmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, \"ls: invalid option -- '%c'\\n\", ch)\n\tfmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, \"Try 'ls --help' for more information.\")\n\tos.Exit(2)\n```\n\nLong flag errors (with some variation):\n```go\ncase arg == \"--\":\n\treturn opts, append(paths, args...)\ncase strings.HasPrefix(arg, \"--\"):\n\tfmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, \"ls: unrecognized option '%s'\\n\", arg)\n\tfmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, \"Try 'ls --help' for more information.\")\n\tos.Exit(2)\n```\n\n### Key Files Demonstrating These Patterns\n\n- `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5312/cmd/version/main.go` - simplest example, just handles --version\n- `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5312/cmd/head/main.go` - comprehensive flag parsing with help/version\n- `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5312/cmd/ls/main.go` - most complex example\n- `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5312/cmd/pwd/main.go` - simpler version with short flag parsing\n- `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5312/cmd/basename/main.go` - flag with arguments example"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4a29dd0e-05c2-41ac-b24f-7283ae52b9aa","uuid":"ab1cfa39-db8e-4f5c-9ae8-4ea3a726afc0","timestamp":"2026-05-19T23:25:58.723Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"In the repo at /private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5312, search for existing implementations of --help and --version flags in cmd/*/main.go files. I want to see the exact pattern used — what the help text looks like, how version is printed, how they handle os.Exit. Search breadth: medium.","agentId":"a4ced3b4ad9458215","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have a comprehensive view of the patterns. Let me create a summary report.\n\n## Summary: --help and --version Flag Implementation Patterns\n\nBased on my analysis of the cmd/*/main.go files in the repository, here are the established patterns for handling --help and --version flags:\n\n### Pattern 1: Constant Help and Version Text (Most Common)\n\nAll commands define help and version strings as package-level constants:\n\n```go\nconst helpText = `Usage: head [OPTION]... [FILE]...\nPrint the first 10 lines of each FILE to standard output.\n...\n      --help        display this help and exit\n      --version     output version information and exit\n`\n\nconst versionText = `head (go-unix-utils) dev\n`\n```\n\n### Pattern 2: Flag Handling Structure\n\nCommands use two flag parsing functions:\n\n1. **parseLongFlag(flag string, remaining []string, opts *options)** - handles --flags\n2. **parseShortFlags(flags string, remaining []string, opts *options)** - handles -f style flags\n\n### Pattern 3: Help and Version Implementation\n\nIn parseLongFlag function:\n\n```go\nfunc parseLongFlag(flag string, remaining []string, opts *options) (int, 